2. QEMU Intel HDA & hda-duplex Architecture Analysis
-----------------------------------------------------
Investigation of QEMU source code (hw/audio/intel-hda.c, hw/audio/hda-codec.c,
hw/audio/hda-codec-common.h) and QEMU audio subsystem (audio/audio.c):

a. Codec Topology in QEMU:
   In hw/audio/hda-codec-common.h, the duplex codec model (duplex_nodes) defines:
     - Node 2: DAC (Digital-to-Analog Converter, output stream index 0).
     - Node 3: Pin Widget (Line Out).
     - Node 4: ADC (Analog-to-Digital Converter, input stream index 1).
     - Node 5: Pin Widget (Line In).
   The connection list for Node 4 is strictly:
     conn = (uint32_t[]) { 5 };
   Node 4 (ADC) connects exclusively to Node 5 (Line In). There is no internal
   multiplexer or connection linking Node 4 (ADC) to Node 2 (DAC) or Node 3 (Line Out).
   Consequently, the emulated codec provides zero hardware/silicon-level internal
   loopback paths.

b. QEMU Audio Backend & Data Flow:
   - Output callback (hda_audio_output_cb): Transfers samples from guest DMA
     memory to QEMU's host audio backend via AUD_write(st->voice.out, ...).
   - Input callback (hda_audio_input_cb): Transfers samples from QEMU's host
     audio backend to guest DMA memory via AUD_read(st->voice.in, ...).
   - In automated test / CI environments (GitHub Actions runners, headless VMs,
     Windows build hosts):
     No physical soundcard or interactive microphone exists. The backend defaults
     to `-audiodev none` (or dummy).
   - Under `-audiodev none`:
     - AUD_write drops all playback samples.
     - AUD_read returns silence (0 bytes / all 0x00s).

c. Implications for Test Truth & Invariants:
   - If a guest driver outputs a known non-zero PCM waveform (e.g. 440 Hz tone
     or deterministic pseudo-random PCM frame) via the HDA output stream DMA,
     the HDA input stream DMA in QEMU will only capture zeroes (silence).
   - A round-trip test asserting received_pcm == transmitted_pcm will always
     fail under QEMU.
   - If a test were relaxed to accept zeroes, it would violate AGENTS.md Rule 1
     and Rule 8 ("Never compare a value to the source it was generated from",
     "Never loosen a threshold to get a green run"): any planted fault changing
     the output sample would still produce zeroes on input, rendering the test
     decorative and incapable of turning red.
   - Using external host file capture (e.g. `-audiodev wav`) would write audio
     files to disk, directly violating the Project Invariant:
     "Camera and audio bytes live in RAM only. No frame, no sample, no derived
      image is written to disk. Only {timestamp, label, confidence} text."

3. Display Coverage: Framebuffer from K2
-----------------------------------------
The work order asks: "Say what the framebuffer from K2 already covers for 'display'.
Do not add display work unless K5 needs it."

The K2 milestone already fully implements and covers all display requirements
for NOVA Nucleus:
- Video Hardware Mode: Limine Framebuffer protocol initializes linear 32-bit
  RGB framebuffer (1024x768 @ 32bpp, pitch=4096, RGB format masks/shifts).
- Graphics Primitive Engine (kernel/src/framebuffer.rs):
  - Direct 32-bit pixel plotting with bounds clamping.
  - Rectangular fill and clearing.
  - 8x8 font rendering engine supporting all 95 printable ASCII glyphs
    (0x20..0x7E), validated against independent font fixtures (tests/test_font_ref.py).
  - Terminal text console with line wrapping, newline handling, scrolling,
    and cursor position management.
- Dual-Firmware Support: Confirmed working identically on both SeaBIOS
  (BIOS / VBE linear framebuffer) and OVMF (UEFI Graphics Output Protocol / GOP).
- Boot & Shell Integration: Framebuffer displays early kernel boot banners,
  diagnostic gate output, and the interactive diagnostic shell.

Because K2 already provides complete linear framebuffer display capabilities,
no additional display driver or work is needed for K5.

